Purpose: how a test shows a catalogue price change for claim current-product-details, when the catalogue in `src/products.ts` is a fixed list with no way to change a price.

## Findings

Tried with Bun 1.4.4 on 2026-11-27, in a scratch project outside this repo that copied the shape of `src/products.ts` and a module importing `getProduct`.

- `mock.module("../src/products", factory)` called inside a test replaces `getProduct` also for a module that imported it before the swap. The importing module returned 3499 before the swap and 3500 after.
- The swap leaks. A second test file, run later in the same `bun test`, read 3500 instead of 3499.
- Restoring works. Keeping a copy of the real module and swapping it back in `afterAll` made the second file read 3499 again.

## Pattern

```ts
import { afterAll, mock, test } from "bun:test";
import * as realProducts from "../src/products";

const original = { ...realProducts };
afterAll(() => {
  mock.module("../src/products", () => original);
});

test("view-items#current-product-details: ...", () => {
  // save the headlamp, then:
  mock.module("../src/products", () => ({
    ...original,
    getProduct: (id: string) => ({ ...original.getProduct(id), priceCents: 3500 }),
  }));
  // view the wishlist and expect 3500
});
```

## Limits

Tried with two test files only, in alphabetical order. The order Bun runs files in was not varied. In this repo `wishlist.test.ts` sorts after the other test files, so a leak would not show today, which is why the restore stays in.

